1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,191
Module 8 is about projects — the feature that stops unrelated work from bleeding into each other.

2
00:00:04,191 --> 00:00:07,000
In this one we create a project and see exactly what it isolates.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,170
A project is a container with its own instructions, its own knowledge bases, and its own enabled tools.

4
00:00:11,170 --> 00:00:13,680
Any chat you start inside it inherits all three automatically.

5
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:17,000
Nothing about it leaks to chats outside the project, and nothing outside leaks in.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,620
To create one, click the plus beside Projects in the sidebar and give it a name.

7
00:00:20,620 --> 00:00:27,000
You land on three tabs — Instructions, Knowledge, and Tools — and whatever you set there applies to every chat you start inside this project.

8
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,970
The worked example: two client projects, Acme and Brightside.

9
00:00:29,970 --> 00:00:36,007
Each gets its own instruction — house style, contact names, pricing — and its own knowledge base of that client's documents.

10
00:00:36,007 --> 00:00:40,000
They never mix, even though you're using the same app, the same day, back to back.

11
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,947
Start a chat inside Acme and it automatically has Acme's instructions and knowledge, no setup needed.

12
00:00:44,947 --> 00:00:50,384
Start a plain chat outside any project and ask about Acme — it sees neither the instructions nor the knowledge.

13
00:00:50,384 --> 00:00:52,000
That boundary is the whole point.

14
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,533
The gotcha: deleting a project does not delete its chats.

15
00:00:54,533 --> 00:00:58,178
They don't vanish — they return to your ungrouped chat list, still fully readable.

16
00:00:58,178 --> 00:01:02,133
What actually gets deleted is the project's memory: the accumulated facts it had learned.

17
00:01:02,133 --> 00:01:04,000
Keep that in mind before you clean one up.

18
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:05,589
Projects give you one instruction layer.

19
00:01:05,589 --> 00:01:10,000
Next we put all three layers — global, project, and chat — side by side and watch them stack in a single reply.

